-## Subtopics
-
-### [Multi-View Density Control Criterion](5663-multi-view-density-control-criterion.md)
-Measures view contribution using multi-view consistency metrics rather than per-view blending weights. Choose when enforcing geometric consistency across views is prioritized over per-view photometric contribution.
-
-### [Complexity-Aware Adaptive Density Control](16126-complexity-aware-adaptive-density-control.md)
-Incorporates local scene complexity and geometry metrics to modulate densification thresholds. Choose when adaptive thresholds based on local surface complexity are required.
+*   **Divergence without Floor:** Without the denominator floor ($1$), Gaussians with $\sum_v \bar{w}_{i,v} \to 0$ (rarely observed) would receive unbounded scores, causing training to diverge [S1, unofficial].
+*   **Occlusion Bias:** Unlike the original 3DGS criterion which weights every view equally regardless of visibility, this criterion weights views by their actual contribution, preventing occluded views from diluting the average [S1, unofficial].
+*   **Comparison to PixelGS:** The raw pixel-count criterion (PixelGS without distance scaling) overdensifies to ~6.6M Gaussians and diverges on large Zip-NeRF scenes. PixelGS with distance scaling suppresses near-object detail because it relies on scene extent, which is unreliable on large scenes [S1, unofficial].
 
 ## Operating characteristics
-*   **Time Complexity:** The criterion requires iterating over all views where the Gaussian contributes and summing per-pixel blending weights [S1, unofficial]. This is $O(N_{pixels})$ per Gaussian per densification step, similar to the original 3D Gaussian Splatting criterion but with slightly more overhead due to weight averaging [S1, unofficial].
-*   **Memory Complexity:** No additional significant memory overhead beyond storing per-view gradient accumulations and blending weight sums [S1, unofficial].
-*   **Performance:** On Zip-NeRF scenes with a 30k iteration schedule, it achieves 25.81 PSNR, 0.830 SSIM, 0.330 LPIPS, 0.106 GMSD using 1.133 million Gaussians in 29.9 minutes [S1, unofficial]. On 210k iterations, it achieves 26.60 PSNR, 0.852 SSIM, 0.282 LPIPS, 0.087 GMSD using 2.495 million Gaussians in 262.9 minutes [S1, unofficial]. At threshold 0.002, it achieves PSNR 28.31, SSIM 0.875, LPIPS 0.160, GMSD 0.073, and Gaussian count 1.79M on the calibration subset [S1, unofficial]. On the Deep Blending dataset (Reset variant), it achieves 29.90 PSNR, 0.902 SSIM, 0.231 LPIPS using 1.656M Gaussians [S1, unofficial]. On the Deep Blending dataset (Decay variant), it achieves 30.03 PSNR, 0.905 SSIM, 0.234 LPIPS using 0.894M Gaussians [S1, unofficial].
-*   **Scalability:** The method is self-limiting and does not require distance scaling guard rails [S1, unofficial]. It stays bounded even at 300k iterations (2.75M Gaussians) while continuing to improve LPIPS and GMSD [S1, unofficial].
-*   **Strengths:** Produces the fewest Gaussians (roughly 55–75% of other criteria's counts) at matched quality [S1, unofficial]. Avoids the instability and over-densification of raw pixel-count criteria [S1, unofficial]. Independent of scene extent, making it robust for large scenes [S1, unofficial].
-*   **Limitations:** Requires calibration of the threshold (0.002) on a subset [S1, unofficial]. The score scale is different from the original 3D Gaussian Splatting criterion, necessitating threshold recalibration [S1, unofficial].
-*   **When to use:** When occlusion is significant, when training stability is a concern (avoiding divergence), and when compactness (fewer Gaussians) is desired without sacrificing quality [S1, unofficial].
-*   **When not to use:** If strict compatibility with the original 3D Gaussian Splatting threshold is required without recalibration [S1, unofficial].
+*   **Time Complexity:** The criterion requires a forward rasterization pass to compute blending weights and a backward pass to compute gradients for each Gaussian per view. This is comparable to the standard 3DGS densification cost but avoids the additional scene extent calculations required by distance scaling [S1, unofficial].
+*   **Memory Complexity:** No additional memory structures are required beyond those used for standard gradient accumulation and blending weight storage [S1, unofficial].
+*   **Measured Performance:**
+    *   **Zip-NeRF (30k iterations):** 25.81 PSNR, 0.830 SSIM, 0.330 LPIPS, 0.106 GMSD, using 1.133 million Gaussians in 29.9 minutes [S1, unofficial].
+    *   **Zip-NeRF (210k iterations):** 26.60 PSNR, 0.852 SSIM, 0.282 LPIPS, 0.087 GMSD, using 2.495 million Gaussians in 262.9 minutes [S1, unofficial].
+    *   **Zip-NeRF (300k iterations):** Gaussian count stays bounded at 2.75M while continuing to improve in LPIPS and GMSD [S1, unofficial].
+    *   **Deep Blending (Reset):** 29.90 PSNR, 0.902 SSIM, 0.231 LPIPS using 1.656M Gaussians [S1, unofficial].
+    *   **Deep Blending (Decay):** 30.03 PSNR, 0.905 SSIM, 0.234 LPIPS using 0.894M Gaussians [S1, unofficial].
+    *   **Calibration Subset (Mip-NeRF360):** At threshold 0.002, achieves PSNR 28.31, SSIM 0.875, LPIPS 0.160, GMSD 0.073, and Gaussian count 1.79M [S1, unofficial].
+*   **Scaling:** The method is intrinsically self-limiting. On large Zip-NeRF scenes, it avoids the catastrophic overdensification seen in PixelGS (which crashes at ~16.8M Gaussians) and maintains a bounded count (~2.75M at 300k iterations) [S1, unofficial].
+*   **Strengths:** Produces the fewest Gaussians (roughly 55–75% of other criteria's counts) at matched quality. It is occlusion-aware without explicit visibility tests and does not rely on unreliable scene extent metrics [S1, unofficial].
+*   **Limitations:** Requires recalibration of the threshold due to the new scale. The criterion is sensitive to the choice of the floor constant, though a value of 1 is robust [S1, unofficial].
 
 ## Background
-The Average Contribution Criterion was introduced in "Counting Contributions, Not Views: Occlusion-Aware Densification for 3D Gaussian Splatting" [S1, unofficial]. It addresses limitations in the original 3D Gaussian Splatting densification criterion, which weights every view equally regardless of visibility, and pixel-count-based criteria (like PixelGS) which entangle view visibility with screen-space footprint [S1, unofficial]. The method was developed to provide a more faithful measure of observation by using mean blending weights, which are independent of coverage and occlusion-aware [S1, unofficial].
+The Average Contribution Criterion was introduced in the paper "Counting Contributions, Not Views: Occlusion-Aware Densification for 3D Gaussian Splatting" [S1, unofficial]. It addresses limitations in the original 3D Gaussian Splatting densification strategy, which averages positional gradients across observed views without accounting for the visibility of those views [S1, unofficial]. The method was developed to improve efficiency and robustness on occlusion-heavy scenes, such as those in the Zip-NeRF dataset [S1, unofficial].
 
